Raise libjuice's UDP receive buffers from 4 KiB to 16 KiB. This, not the DTLS
buffer, is what actually capped the usable SCTP path MTU.

Each of libjuice's three connection backends reads datagrams into a fixed stack
buffer sized by its own file-local `BUFFER_SIZE`:

    src/conn_poll.c:20     #define BUFFER_SIZE 4096   <- the default backend
    src/conn_thread.c:19   #define BUFFER_SIZE 4096
    src/conn_mux.c:20      #define BUFFER_SIZE 4096

`recvfrom()` does not truncate-and-continue for a datagram: whatever does not fit
the supplied buffer is **discarded**, and the excess is gone. So a datagram larger
than 4096 does not arrive short, it does not arrive at all -- and nothing logs it,
because from the socket's point of view a read succeeded.

**Measured, and that measurement is why this patch exists in this form.** The
companion `libdatachannel-dtls-buffer.patch` raises libdatachannel's own 4096-byte
DTLS receive buffer to 16 KiB, which A11's findings had identified as the ceiling
on `rtc::Configuration::mtu`. With *only* that patch applied, on both reference
machines, an MTU of 4132 still worked and 4256 still carried nothing -- the cliff
had not moved at all. The DTLS buffer was a real artificial limit and was not the
binding one; libjuice sits below it and had the same number.

With both patches the cliff is gone and the curve is monotonic to the top of the
range A11's search will consider. Bare `raw-webrtc`, 64 KiB pipelined, machine A:

    MTU    before        with both patches
    4132   184 MiB/s     184
    6144   nothing       238
    8192   nothing       318
    9216   nothing       348

and the 64 KiB round trip 747us -> 409us. So both patches are required and neither
is sufficient. The DTLS one is still correct on its own terms -- it is the value
handed to the TLS implementation as its MTU -- but it buys nothing until a datagram
that size can reach it.

16 KiB to match the DTLS buffer, so the two layers cannot disagree about the
largest record that can cross. These are stack buffers in the receive loop of a
connection thread, so 16 KiB is unremarkable there.

The send path needs nothing: `conn_poll_send` hands the caller's buffer straight to
`udp_sendto` with no fixed intermediate.

diff --git a/src/conn_mux.c b/src/conn_mux.c
index 6af8833..3818a8d 100644
--- a/src/conn_mux.c
+++ b/src/conn_mux.c
@@ -17,7 +17,12 @@
 #include <assert.h>
 #include <string.h>
 
-#define BUFFER_SIZE 4096
+// Big enough for the largest DTLS record libdatachannel will read (16 KiB, see
+// scripts/patches/libdatachannel-dtls-buffer.patch). recvfrom() discards whatever
+// does not fit, so a datagram larger than this is not truncated at the application
+// layer -- it is silently destroyed, which is what capped the usable SCTP path MTU
+// at ~4150 whatever the DTLS layer was told.
+#define BUFFER_SIZE 16384
 #define INITIAL_MAP_SIZE 16
 
